<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Truce of Andrusovo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1667">1667</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-polish-war-1654-1667">Russo-Polish War (1654–1667)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Truce of Andrusovo, agreed in January 1667 in a village near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>, ended the Russo-Polish War of 1654–1667. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> ceded Smolensk and left-bank <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> to the Tsardom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, which also received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, nominally for two years, while right-bank Ukraine stayed Polish. The division of Ukraine along the Dnieper was confirmed by the Eternal Peace of 1686.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/truce-of-andrusovo.jpg" alt="Truce of Andrusovo" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mathiasrex Maciej Szczepańczyk, based on layers of Halibutt · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Truce_of_Andrusovo_1667.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The truce was signed on 9 February 1667, which was 30 January by the old-style reckoning in the village of Andrusovo, not far from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>, by Afanasy Ordin-Nashchokin for the Tsardom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and Jerzy Chlebowicz for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. Representatives of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate</a> were not allowed to take part. It set a truce of thirteen and a half years, during which both states were obliged to prepare the conditions for eternal peace.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> secured Left-bank Ukraine, the Siever lands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>. The Commonwealth kept Right-bank Ukraine and Russian-occupied Belarus, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitebsk">Vitebsk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> and Dzwinsk. Kiev, though on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, was handed to Russia for two years, a transfer phrased as temporary but in fact made permanent in 1686 in exchange for 146,000 rubles. The Zaporozhian Sich became a condominium of both states, and both agreed to common defence against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and to free trade. Russia was to pay the Commonwealth 1,000,000 złotych, or 200,000 rubles, for Left-bank Ukraine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The transfer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> had far-reaching consequences. Kiev was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> metropolitan, who kept authority over the Orthodox population of the Commonwealth's eastern lands, and it hosted the Mohyla Academy, which opened Orthodoxy to Western influence. It had been an Orthodox counterweight to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> patriarchate, founded in 1589. The transfer came only days after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patriarch-nikon">Patriarch Nikon</a> won the upper hand over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avvakum">Avvakum</a>, which led to the schism between the Reformed Orthodoxy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-believers">Old Believers</a>. Kiev now gave the Russian patriarch an academy whose scholars Nikon had already relied on for his reforms.</p><p>As the metropolitan's see, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> also gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> influence over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> of Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and protection of that population later became an argument for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov">Romanov</a> influence over eastern Poland-Lithuania. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> the treaty is often seen as leading to the partition of the Hetmanate between its stronger neighbours. Polish opinion considers it a significant mistake that tipped the balance of power and replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> as the dominant state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> with the emerging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Truce of Andrusovo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Thirteen Years’ War of 1654–1667, after the Pereiaslav agreement with the Ukrainian Cossacks, Russia regained Smolensk and won Kyiv and left-bank Ukraine by the <b>Truce of Andrusovo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
